ype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder (Engine 1: DCF + Multiples)**\n\nReasons for archetype selection:\n1. Subscription revenue is ~95% of total revenue; gross margin 81.6% [Actual FY26 TTM]; this is a recurring-revenue software business, not a hardware hybrid.\n2. Revenue CAGR 23.5% over 5 fiscal years [Actual], with NRR 110% and mid-90s gross retention [Estimate \u2014 company-prepared remarks, Q1 FY27 call].\n3. Expanding profitability: GAAP operating margin 8.0% (FY23) \u2192 12.1% (FY26); non-GAAP op. margin 30% (FY26) [Actual].\n4. FCF positive and net-cash balance sheet; FCF margin 26% (FY26) [Actual].\n5. The customer base and switching costs are high: platform embedded in production environments, 16 consecutive Gartner MQ Leadership [Actual \u2014 company press release].\n\nEngine selection rationale:\n- Engine 4 (expectation-driven) would over-value the AI-observability optionality and is not required \u2014 the current business already carries the value.\n- Engine 3 (hybrid) is inappropriate: no meaningful hardware installed base.\n- Engine 1 with an explicit owner-earnings definition is appropriate, because the company is a quality compounder but GAAP earnings are suppressed by SBC, so a cash-flow definition must be built deliberately (Section 2.5).\n\nTransition status: **None**. The company is not transitioning between archetypes; the DPS pricing shift is a monetization model evolution within the same archetype.\n\nI declare the data pack adequate for this analysis, with four critical gaps I must carry:\n- **[Unconfirmed]** \u2014 R&D is not in the pack; I sourced a third-party estimate (TTM Jun-2026 R&D $0.502B, Macrotrends [Estimate]).\n- **[Unconfirmed]** \u2014 Arize acquisition ($915M, announced Aug-2026) terms/funding not established.\n- **[Unconfirmed]** \u2014 Net debt components: our long-term debt column only; I have reconstructed a balance-sheet net cash figure with named components.\n- **[Unconfirmed]** \u2014 FY27 GAAP OCF is not directly guided; I have estimated it from adjusted FCF guidance.\n\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check (as of T0, Aug-2026)**\n\n| Variable | Level / Direction | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10-yr Treasury | 4.74% (Aug 21), elevated, upward drift | [Actual] \u2014 Fed H.15, 2026-08-24 |\n| 2-yr Treasury | 4.24% | [Actual] \u2014 Fed H.15 |\n| 5-yr TIPS real yield | 2.09% | [Actual] \u2014 Fed H.15 |\n| 10-yr TIPS real yield | 2.40% | [Actual] \u2014 Fed H.15 |\n| Fed funds effective | 3.63% | [Actual] \u2014 Fed H.15 |\n| DXY | Directionally firm in 2026 (DT guidance cites $19M FY27 revenue FX headwind and $23M ARR headwind) | [Estimate] \u2014 company guidance implies dollar strength |\n| Inflation breakeven (10y) | ~2.3% | [Arithmetic] \u2014 nominal minus TIPS |\n| Credit spreads | Not sourced; no material DT credit risk | [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not a governing input |\n| Fed stance | Policy rate 3.63% vs ~2.3% breakeven \u2192 real policy rate ~1.3%, mildly restrictive; market is pricing normalization/cuts | [Estimate] \u2014 inferred from H.15; FOMC statement not retrieved |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Classification**\n\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goldilocks | 35% | Enterprise software demand remains solid (DT record new-logo growth +160% YoY, Q1 FY27 [Actual]); 10-yr high at 4.7% but policy rate is already 3.63% |\n| Reflation | 40% | Growth is still above trend and rates are elevated; value/cyclicals favored, but DT can still compound |\n| Stagflation | 15% | 10-yr real yield 2.4% + sticky inflation + FX volatility |\n| Recession | 10% | Lagged effects of 30-yr at 5.3%; late-cycle risk for IT budgets |\n| **Total** | **100%** | |\n\n**Step 1-3. Enterprise/Sector Macro Sensitivity**\n\n| Axis | Score (-3 to +3) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest-rate sensitivity | -1 | Long-duration equity multiple, but DT is net-cash and has minimal debt |\n| Dollar sensitivity | -2 | ~40% of revenue in foreign currency; FY27 guidance includes FX headwind; every ~4-5% dollar rise trims ~1% revenue |\n| Liquidity sensitivity | +1 | High FCF self-funding; still exposed to software-multiple compression |\n| Economic-cycle sensitivity | 0 | Observability is a budget line that gets scrutinized in downturns, but AI workloads are a priority (AI customers consume at 1.5x non-AI [Actual]) |\n| Commodity sensitivity | 0 | Negligible |\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment**\nWeighted regime distribution \u00d7 sensitivities \u2192 **Net Headwind** (driven mostly by FX and the long-duration multiple against a 4.7% risk-free rate).\n*Conviction adjustment: -0.5 steps.* Entry execution should favor limit orders and tiered scaling, not a single market purchase.\n\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n- Top-tier mission-critical software company with $2B+ ARR, NRR 110%, 81% gross margin \u2192 5-year survival probability: **~85-90%** [Estimate \u2014 base rate].\n- Probability such a company sustains double-digit FCF growth over 5 years: **~40-50%** [Estimate \u2014 base rate].\n- Success rate of large consumption-based pricing transitions (term\u2192consumption): **~20-30%** [Estimate \u2014 base rate for platform transitions].\n- Probability an M&A at $915M generates ROIC>WACC by year 5: **~25-35%** [Estimate \u2014 large-deal base rate].\n- Probability of reaching $10B ARR from $2.1B in ~5-6 years (implied by bull case): **~5-10%** [Estimate].\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate and Justification**\nMy base-case model implies DT sustains 13-15% revenue growth for 3-4 years and stabilizes with 20%+ owner-earnings margins. That's above the 40-50% base rate for a compounder, but I only require a 50% probability of the base case. Company-specific supporting evidence:\n1. 16 consecutive years as a Gartner MQ Leader [Actual] \u2014 durable category leadership.\n2. Trailing 12-month organic net-new ARR accelerated for four consecutive quarters [Actual] \u2014 momentum inflection.\n3. Log management annualized consumption nearly doubled in two quarters to $200M [Actual], showing the DPS consumption engine actually works.\n4. NRR 110% and gross retention mid-90s [Estimate] \u2014 expansion engine intact.\n5. Record new-logo growth >160%, with $285K average land [Actual] \u2014 broad demand.\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\nMy bull case (25%) implies a ~$60+ equity with $5B+ FY32 revenue. That is within ~2x the base rate for category leaders, justified by the data above. No red flag.\n\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)**\nDynatrace sells an AI-powered observability platform that helps large enterprises monitor, secure, and automate their software environments. It ingests metrics, logs, traces, and security data into its Grail data lakehouse and uses Davis AI to provide causal AI/automation. The company is shifting from per-unit licensing to consumption-based DPS pricing, meaning revenue grows as customers' telemetry volumes grow \u2014 especially from AI workloads.\n\n**Understandability: Medium.** The high-level model is straightforward, but the switching-cost/hard-trust dynamics require enterprise-software experience. Rating: Medium \u2014 no downgrade.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Direction**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand/tech) | 2 | Stable | Gartner Leader 16y; proprietary OneAgent/Grail/Davis |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Stable | Embedded in production; NRR 110%; mid-90s gross retention |\n| Network effects | 1 | Narrowing/Stable | Benchmarking data improves models, but not a multiplayer network |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Narrowing | NewRelic 3-5x cheaper at scale; open-source alternatives free |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Stable | R&D scale grows; cloud costs scale with consumption |\n| Regulatory barriers | 1 | Stable | No license moat; compliance overhead |\n| Data moat | 2 | Widening | More telemetry \u2192 better Davis AI; but OpenTelemetry standardizes data formats |\n| **Total** | **12/21** | **Net: Stable** | Wide moat, low end |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification**\n**Fast Grower**, transitioning toward a **Stalwart** as growth normalizes from ~20% toward ~12-15%. Key checkpoint for fast growers: growth must persist; for stalwarts: NRR and margins. Both checkpoints currently pass.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth FY23-26 CAGR | +19.7% | [Arithmetic] \u2014 filed |\n| Last-4Q revenue growth | +16.2% YoY | [Arithmetic] \u2014 filed |\n| Operating margin FY26 | 12.1% GAAP; 30% non-GAAP | [Actual] \u2014 filed / press release |\n| ROE, TTM | 6.2% (163/2611) | [Arithmetic] |\n| FCF margin FY26 | 26.3% (530/2018) | [Arithmetic] \u2014 filed |\n|